%doc """
    The moves inside one explanatory episode, as URG analyses them.
    The grammar describes observed episodes, so a rejected episode is
    evidence against the grammar. Move labels are provisional, and they
    use the words of the URG analysis.
    The rules follow the URG patterns "failing case motivates fix",
    "simplest case first" and "refinement of an earlier solution".
    """ ;

%start episode ;

episode "One explanatory episode."
    = "refers back"? "states the problem" development generalisation? ;

development """
    The development of the episode: graded cases,
    or a first solution and its refinements.
    """
    = progression
    | "proposes a solution" refinement
    ;

progression "The simplest case first, then complications and repairs."
    = "shows the simplest case" ("adds a complication" | fix)* ;

fix "A failing case motivates a repair."
    = "shows a failing case" "states the principle"? "gives a remedy" ;

refinement "Each revision closes one gap, and the revised solution can show a new gap."
    = "identifies a gap" "revises the solution" refinement? ;

generalisation """
    Hypothesis under test: authors give at least two examples
    before they generalise.
    """
    = "gives an example"{2,} "generalises the example" "names the new concept"? ;
